Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> Doug Freyburger <dfrey...@yahoo.com> wrote:
>> Janis Papanagnou wrote:
>
>> I name gems like "hard green lev 4"
>
> Is there any real point to classifying and carrying
> a bunch of gems around that early in the game?

The lesson of how to read the source code and the spoilers and then do
analysis on it to build tables and processes is worth learning. 
Building a process for gem identification is something I did at some
point to help learn analysis.  I still use the resulting process as a
matter of course in some games but I now rarely go farther than noting
hard gems by color, glass by color and thus soft valuable gems by color.

Glass gems make acceptable missiles.  They do rock damage at 1/10th the
weight.  It's an item that I would be quick to replace with a good
dagger but the value of knowing which gems are glass is not zero.

> I've been leaving them where they are until at
> least mid-game, by which time I am stronger and
> can carry more and have a blessed bag of holding

I do tend to carry all non-rock items discovered on each level to a
stash near the down stairs of that level. Some items I shuffle down
some I don't. What's worth shuttling down varies by level of the game.
While shops are still valuable in my game I tend to shuttle all of the
gems I don't yet know are glass. Later they get left behind in the
per-level stashes.

> What is the reasoning behind carrying them
> around sooner (other than the one hard gem
> for engraving mentioned below)?

Valuable gems have more cost per weight than gold.  Valuable gems
can be thrown to unicorns.  All gems do rock damage when thrown/fired
at 1/10th the weight of rocks. One hard gem as an engraver continues
to be worth carrying all the way to astral. All but the last one
eventually drop off worth doing.  But in the early game the resources
available are so few.

>> I like to carry a hard gem with me as an engraver
>
> Now that makes sense.
> I may have to think about doing that.
>
> But still, you only need one for that, right?

Exactly.  For me it's the first hard gem I enounter in the game.  I
#name it "engraver" and keep it for the rest of the game.  As Janas
pointed out sometimes I'll keep a ring instead.

>> might or might not bother getting exact
>> identities after I know all 9 glass types.
>
> I eventually identify them all collaterally,
> merely by holding a handful of them in my
> inventory each time I happen to be reading
> a blessed scroll of identify for some other
> reason.

When shops are still worth looting you don't want to fully identify
glass.  Better to get a type back from a unicorn toss and be certain of
its identity.  Fully identified glass is ignored by all shopkeepers but
they can't do that with glass that you just called glass.  So I also
eventually end up identifying them but only long after it's no longer
worth looting shops.

> I don't make special trips just for gems,
> but if I'm at a stash when I read a blessed
> scroll of identify then I grab whichever
> gems are there (along with any other
> unidentified stuff, and wands and tools
> whose charge count I'd like to know)
> before reading it,

I'll keep one of each type in a container on the way down.  Maybe I'll
have a stack of rubies or whatever still with me by the time I reach
astral just to increase score.  Gold is the least weight efficient, then
valuable gems, then artifacts.  Weight for weight some gems beat some
artifacts.  As a hint that fact says much about the worth of the
artifacts in question!  That's an aspect that effects game play more
than final scores.

